      "problem": "Find the exact solution to $3\\ln (5x+1)-2 = 13.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1)-2 & =13 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1) & =15 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the natural logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the natural logarithm is $e,$ so we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1) & =5 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1)} & ={e}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $e^{\\ln (5x+1)} = 5x+1.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+1 & ={e}^{5} \\\\ 5x & ={e}^{5}-1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{{e}^{5}-1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
